      {
        "id": "s3_canal",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Haversian canal and lamellae",
        "marks": 1,
        "why": "The canal is the centre of the unit and every other part is described by its distance from it, so it is described first.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the lamellae surround the marrow cavity. They surround the Haversian canal.",
          "Leaving out the word concentric. The rings sit one inside another."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Think of a tree trunk cut across: the canal is the centre and the lamellae are the growth rings.",
        "margin_note": "Two parts, plus what the canal carries. The word concentric must appear.",
        "mark_note": "Canal and lamellae",
        "lines": [
          "1) Haversian canal: a narrow canal running along",
          "the length of the bone, parallel to the marrow",
          "cavity.",
          "It carries an artery, a vein, a lymph vessel and",
          "a nerve.",
          "2) Concentric lamellae: rings of bone matrix",
          "laid one inside another around the canal."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_lacunae",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Lacunae and canaliculi",
        "marks": 1,
        "why": "Bone matrix is solid, so the cells buried in it can be reached only through these fine canals; the canaliculi are the reason an osteocyte stays alive.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the canaliculi carry blood. They carry tissue fluid and the cell processes, not blood.",
          "Placing the lacunae inside the Haversian canal. They lie between the lamellae."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "A lacuna is a small lake holding one cell; the canaliculi are the little channels joining the lakes.",
        "margin_note": "Say what each space holds. Both names are marked, and so is the osteocyte.",
        "mark_note": "Lacunae and canaliculi",
        "lines": [
          "3) Lacunae: small fluid-filled spaces lying",
          "between the lamellae.",
          "Each lacuna holds an osteocyte, a mature bone",
          "cell.",
          "4) Canaliculi: very fine canals joining the",
          "lacunae to one another and to the Haversian",
          "canal.",
          "The processes of the osteocytes run inside",
          "them."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_volkmann",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Volkmann's canals",
        "marks": 1,
        "why": "The Haversian canals all run lengthwise, so a set of cross canals is needed to link them; this book's answer states only the link to the marrow cavity, while standard anatomy also has them joining neighbouring Haversian canals and the periosteum.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that Volkmann's canals run lengthwise. They run across, joining the lengthwise canals.",
          "Leaving out the transport function, which is the point of the whole system."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Haversian canals run along the bone; Volkmann's canals run across and join them, like the rungs of a ladder.",
        "margin_note": "Name the canals, say what they connect, then close with the transport function.",
        "mark_note": "Volkmann's canals",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Volkmann's canals join one osteon to the next",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "5) Volkmann's canals run across the bone.",
          "They connect one Haversian canal with another,",
          "and with the periosteum and the marrow cavity.",
          "Blood vessels pass through them, so the whole",
          "system carries food and oxygen to the bone",
          "cells."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "insider_note": "Five named parts carry this answer, and the two most often dropped are the canaliculi and Volkmann's canals — number the parts 1 to 5 so the examiner can count them at a glance."
}
